1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,317
Aerogel is ninety-nine percent air by
volume, yet it is a solid you can hold.

2
00:00:05,317 --> 00:00:10,869
The material is silicon dioxide, the same
compound in window glass, but arranged as

3
00:00:10,869 --> 00:00:15,083
a skeletal network of strands each two to
five nanometers wide.

4
00:00:15,083 --> 00:00:20,010
What makes it aerogel is not the chemistry
— it is what happens when you remove the

5
00:00:20,010 --> 00:00:23,632
liquid from a wet gel without letting the
structure collapse.

6
00:00:23,632 --> 00:00:25,216
What is aerogel made of?

7
00:00:25,216 --> 00:00:28,859
You start with a solution of silicon
alkoxide in alcohol.

8
00:00:28,859 --> 00:00:33,844
When water is added with an acid or base
catalyst, hydrolysis and condensation

9
00:00:33,844 --> 00:00:37,168
reactions link silicon atoms through
oxygen bridges.

10
00:00:37,168 --> 00:00:43,019
The solution transitions to a gel — a
continuous solid network suspended in

11
00:00:43,019 --> 00:00:47,707
At this stage the material is called a wet
gel or alcogel.

12
00:00:47,707 --> 00:00:52,576
The silica skeleton is already formed, but
the pores are full of solvent.

13
00:00:52,576 --> 00:00:56,913
If you simply evaporate that solvent,
capillary forces pull the strands together

14
00:00:56,913 --> 00:00:59,461
and the structure collapses into a dense
solid.

15
00:00:59,461 --> 00:01:04,641
Aerogel exists only if you remove the
liquid without crossing a liquid-vapor

16
00:01:04,641 --> 00:01:05,323
interface.

17
00:01:05,323 --> 00:01:08,103
The defining step is supercritical drying.

18
00:01:08,103 --> 00:01:13,662
The wet gel is placed in an autoclave and
the solvent is exchanged for liquid carbon

19
00:01:13,662 --> 00:01:14,192
dioxide.

20
00:01:14,192 --> 00:01:18,717
The autoclave is then heated above
thirty-one degrees Celsius and pressurized

21
00:01:18,717 --> 00:01:20,597
above seventy-three atmospheres.

22
00:01:20,597 --> 00:01:26,610
Under these conditions carbon dioxide
becomes supercritical — it has no distinct

23
00:01:26,610 --> 00:01:28,264
liquid or vapor phase.

24
00:01:28,264 --> 00:01:33,336
There is no meniscus, no surface tension,
and therefore no capillary force to

25
00:01:33,336 --> 00:01:34,456
collapse the gel.

26
00:01:34,456 --> 00:01:39,153
When the supercritical fluid is vented,
the silica network remains intact.

27
00:01:39,153 --> 00:01:44,040
This process was first demonstrated by
Samuel Kistler in nineteen thirty-one.

28
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:48,729
He published the method in Nature,
comparing aerogels of silica, alumina, and

29
00:01:48,729 --> 00:01:53,723
other oxides, all prepared by replacing
the liquid phase with air while preserving

30
00:01:53,723 --> 00:01:54,819
the gel structure.

31
00:01:54,819 --> 00:01:59,744
Transmission electron microscopy shows
that aerogel is not a uniform foam.

32
00:01:59,744 --> 00:02:01,608
It is a network of clusters.

33
00:02:01,608 --> 00:02:07,111
During gelation, silica polymerizes into
primary particles two to five nanometers

34
00:02:07,111 --> 00:02:07,587
across.

35
00:02:07,587 --> 00:02:11,161
These particles aggregate into branching
chains and clusters.

36
00:02:11,161 --> 00:02:15,966
The clusters connect to form a continuous
network with pores twenty to one hundred

37
00:02:15,966 --> 00:02:16,904
nanometers wide.

38
00:02:16,904 --> 00:02:22,105
The structure is fractal at small scales —
if you zoom in on a strand, it reveals the

39
00:02:22,105 --> 00:02:23,512
same clustered texture.

40
00:02:23,512 --> 00:02:28,142
The strands themselves are ten to twenty
nanometers thick where multiple particle

41
00:02:28,142 --> 00:02:29,171
chains have fused.

42
00:02:29,171 --> 00:02:33,883
This is why aerogel scatters
short-wavelength light and appears blue in

43
00:02:33,883 --> 00:02:37,400
transmission, even though silica itself is
colorless.

44
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:41,949
The scattering follows Rayleigh's law, the
same mechanism that colors the sky.

45
00:02:41,949 --> 00:02:46,932
The nanoscale architecture determines
every bulk property — density, thermal

46
00:02:46,932 --> 00:02:52,046
conductivity, mechanical strength, and
optical behavior all trace back to this

47
00:02:52,046 --> 00:02:53,816
clustered network geometry.

48
00:02:53,816 --> 00:02:58,884
Aerogel density ranges from about zero
point zero zero three grams per cubic

49
00:02:58,884 --> 00:03:02,685
centimeter to zero point five grams per
cubic centimeter.

50
00:03:02,685 --> 00:03:07,930
For comparison, air at sea level is
roughly zero point zero zero one two grams

51
00:03:07,930 --> 00:03:13,108
per cubic centimeter, and solid silica
glass is two point two grams per cubic

52
00:03:13,108 --> 00:03:13,848
centimeter.

53
00:03:13,848 --> 00:03:18,233
The density is set by the concentration of
the precursor solution.

54
00:03:18,233 --> 00:03:22,419
A dilute solution produces a sparse
network with high porosity.

55
00:03:22,419 --> 00:03:27,706
A concentrated solution yields a denser
aerogel with smaller pores and thicker

56
00:03:27,706 --> 00:03:28,248
strands.

57
00:03:28,248 --> 00:03:33,488
Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory has
produced aerogels at the extreme low end,

58
00:03:33,488 --> 00:03:35,859
approaching the density of air itself.

59
00:03:35,859 --> 00:03:40,379
These materials are used as particle
collectors in space missions because a

60
00:03:40,379 --> 00:03:43,693
high-speed dust grain embeds itself
without shattering.

61
00:03:43,693 --> 00:03:48,914
I wanted the lowest-density record to be
one clean number — 'the lightest solid

62
00:03:48,914 --> 00:03:50,301
ever made.' It isn't.

63
00:03:50,301 --> 00:03:54,052
Different measurement methods give
different lowest values, so I kept the

64
00:03:54,052 --> 00:03:56,621
range instead of quoting the most dramatic
figure.

65
00:03:56,621 --> 00:04:00,659
Aerogel is one of the best thermal
insulators among solids.

66
00:04:00,659 --> 00:04:05,929
Its thermal conductivity can be as low as
zero point zero one three watts per

67
00:04:05,929 --> 00:04:11,472
meter-kelvin in air at room temperature,
compared to zero point zero two four for

68
00:04:11,472 --> 00:04:15,373
still air and zero point zero four for
polyurethane foam.

69
00:04:15,373 --> 00:04:17,204
Three mechanisms contribute.

70
00:04:17,204 --> 00:04:22,564
First, the silica skeleton is sparse and
tortuous, so conduction through the solid

71
00:04:22,564 --> 00:04:23,283
is minimal.

72
00:04:23,283 --> 00:04:27,510
Second, the pores are smaller than the
mean free path of air molecules at

73
00:04:27,510 --> 00:04:30,637
atmospheric pressure, which is about sixty
nanometers.

74
00:04:30,637 --> 00:04:35,219
Gas molecules collide with pore walls more
often than with each other, suppressing

75
00:04:35,219 --> 00:04:37,621
convection and reducing gaseous
conduction.

76
00:04:37,621 --> 00:04:43,147
Third, the material is mostly transparent
to infrared at short path lengths, though

77
00:04:43,147 --> 00:04:47,141
radiative transfer becomes significant at
high temperatures.

78
00:04:47,141 --> 00:04:52,536
This combination makes aerogel useful in
applications from Mars rover insulation to

79
00:04:52,536 --> 00:04:54,421
deep-sea instrument housings.

80
00:04:54,421 --> 00:04:55,757
Aerogel is brittle.

81
00:04:55,757 --> 00:05:01,239
Compressive strength ranges from about
zero point zero two megapascals for the

82
00:05:01,239 --> 00:05:05,597
lowest-density samples to ten megapascals
for denser variants.

83
00:05:05,597 --> 00:05:09,676
Tensile strength is lower, often below
zero point one megapascals.

84
00:05:09,676 --> 00:05:14,867
The material fractures rather than deforms
plastically because the silica network is

85
00:05:14,867 --> 00:05:18,637
held together by covalent bonds in a
sparse, rigid framework.

86
00:05:18,637 --> 00:05:21,117
There is no ductile phase to absorb
energy.

87
00:05:21,117 --> 00:05:25,558
Despite this fragility, aerogel can
support thousands of times its own weight

88
00:05:25,558 --> 00:05:27,115
if the load is distributed.

89
00:05:27,115 --> 00:05:32,087
A one-gram aerogel brick can hold a
kilogram without visible deformation.

90
00:05:32,087 --> 00:05:37,332
The structure fails when local stress
exceeds the bond strength of a critical

91
00:05:37,332 --> 00:05:40,805
cluster, at which point a crack propagates
rapidly.

92
00:05:40,805 --> 00:05:45,999
The brittleness is a direct consequence of
the open architecture — the same geometry

93
00:05:45,999 --> 00:05:50,759
that gives aerogel its low density and low
thermal conductivity also makes it

94
00:05:50,759 --> 00:05:51,872
mechanically weak.

95
00:05:51,872 --> 00:05:57,687
Silica aerogel is the most studied, but
the supercritical drying method applies to

96
00:05:57,687 --> 00:05:58,821
other materials.

97
00:05:58,821 --> 00:06:03,595
Alumina aerogels are made from aluminum
alkoxide precursors and can withstand

98
00:06:03,595 --> 00:06:05,579
higher temperatures than silica.

99
00:06:05,579 --> 00:06:09,851
Carbon aerogels are synthesized from
organic gels, such as

100
00:06:09,851 --> 00:06:15,524
resorcinol-formaldehyde, followed by
pyrolysis to convert the polymer network

101
00:06:15,524 --> 00:06:16,997
to graphitic carbon.

102
00:06:16,997 --> 00:06:21,899
These have electrical conductivity and are
used in supercapacitors and fuel cells.

103
00:06:21,899 --> 00:06:27,649
Polymer aerogels, including polyimide and
polyurethane variants, offer flexibility

104
00:06:27,649 --> 00:06:29,893
and can be cast into thin films.

105
00:06:29,893 --> 00:06:34,858
NASA has developed polymer aerogels for
inflatable structures and dust capture.

106
00:06:34,858 --> 00:06:39,886
Each variant shares the defining trait: a
nanoporous solid network with porosity

107
00:06:39,886 --> 00:06:44,725
above ninety percent, produced by removing
the liquid phase without collapse.

108
00:06:44,725 --> 00:06:47,170
Unmodified silica aerogel is hydrophilic.

109
00:06:47,170 --> 00:06:51,999
Surface hydroxyl groups bond with water
molecules, and absorbed moisture degrades

110
00:06:51,999 --> 00:06:56,053
thermal insulation and can cause
structural collapse if liquid water

111
00:06:56,053 --> 00:06:57,424
condenses in the pores.

112
00:06:57,424 --> 00:07:02,696
To prevent this, aerogels are often
treated with a silane coupling agent, such

113
00:07:02,696 --> 00:07:06,954
as trimethylchlorosilane or
hexamethyldisilazane, either during

114
00:07:06,954 --> 00:07:09,184
solvent exchange or after drying.

115
00:07:09,184 --> 00:07:14,281
The silane reacts with hydroxyl groups on
the silica surface, replacing them with

116
00:07:14,281 --> 00:07:15,728
nonpolar methyl groups.

117
00:07:15,728 --> 00:07:20,515
The treated aerogel becomes hydrophobic —
water beads on its surface rather than

118
00:07:20,515 --> 00:07:21,173
wetting it.

119
00:07:21,173 --> 00:07:26,599
This modification is essential for outdoor
applications and for aerogels used in

120
00:07:26,599 --> 00:07:27,888
humid environments.

121
00:07:27,888 --> 00:07:32,695
The treatment does not significantly alter
the pore structure or thermal properties,

122
00:07:32,695 --> 00:07:36,757
but it extends the material's useful
lifetime in real-world conditions.

123
00:07:36,757 --> 00:07:42,428
The material is not exotic in composition
— it is silicon dioxide, the second most

124
00:07:42,428 --> 00:07:44,848
abundant compound in Earth's crust.

125
00:07:44,848 --> 00:07:50,560
What makes aerogel unusual is the
preservation of a gel's structure in a dry

126
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:55,154
The method is reproducible and well
documented, but it requires equipment

127
00:07:55,154 --> 00:07:58,427
capable of reaching supercritical
conditions safely.

128
00:07:58,427 --> 00:08:02,722
The resulting material is
counterintuitive: a solid that is mostly

129
00:08:02,722 --> 00:08:08,058
empty space, an insulator that looks like
frozen smoke, a brittle ceramic that can

130
00:08:08,058 --> 00:08:09,035
be translucent.

131
00:08:09,035 --> 00:08:13,424
The properties follow directly from the
structure, and the structure follows from

132
00:08:13,424 --> 00:08:14,075
the process.

133
00:08:14,075 --> 00:08:18,314
The lowest reported densities for aerogel
vary slightly depending on measurement

134
00:08:18,314 --> 00:08:19,904
method and sample preparation.

135
00:08:19,904 --> 00:08:24,765
Some sources cite zero point zero zero one
grams per cubic centimeter, others zero

136
00:08:24,765 --> 00:08:29,330
point zero zero three, and some report
values as high as zero point zero zero

137
00:08:29,330 --> 00:08:32,709
five grams per cubic centimeter for the
lightest samples.

138
00:08:32,709 --> 00:08:38,548
The variation reflects differences in how
density is measured — whether by geometric

139
00:08:38,548 --> 00:08:42,997
volume or by helium pycnometry, which
accounts for closed pores.

140
00:08:42,997 --> 00:08:47,429
The structural details at the nanometer
scale also depend on imaging technique.

141
00:08:47,429 --> 00:08:52,913
Transmission electron microscopy requires
thin sections and can introduce artifacts.

142
00:08:52,913 --> 00:08:57,939
Small-angle X-ray scattering provides
statistical information about pore size

143
00:08:57,939 --> 00:09:00,224
distribution but not direct images.

144
00:09:00,224 --> 00:09:05,272
The overall picture is consistent, but
precise values for the lowest density or

145
00:09:05,272 --> 00:09:10,320
smallest pore size should be understood as
representative rather than absolute.

146
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:14,775
Aerogel appears translucent or milky
rather than transparent, even though

147
00:09:14,775 --> 00:09:16,789
silica itself is optically clear.

148
00:09:16,789 --> 00:09:19,745
The difference is scattering.

149
00:09:19,745 --> 00:09:27,694
Light passing through aerogel encounters
density fluctuations at the nanometer

150
00:09:27,694 --> 00:09:34,523
scale — the boundaries between silica
strands and air-filled pores.

151
00:09:34,523 --> 00:09:38,455
When the characteristic size of these
features is comparable to the wavelength

152
00:09:38,455 --> 00:09:40,875
of visible light, Rayleigh scattering
dominates.

153
00:09:40,875 --> 00:09:46,201
Shorter wavelengths scatter more strongly
than longer ones, following an inverse

154
00:09:46,201 --> 00:09:47,333
fourth-power law.

155
00:09:47,333 --> 00:09:53,005
This is why thin aerogel samples appear
blue in transmitted light and yellowish in

156
00:09:53,005 --> 00:09:54,112
reflected light.

157
00:09:54,112 --> 00:09:58,077
Thicker samples scatter so much light that
they appear opaque white.

158
00:09:58,077 --> 00:10:02,741
The effect is quantified by the extinction
coefficient, which for silica aerogel

159
00:10:02,741 --> 00:10:06,939
ranges from ten to one hundred inverse
centimeters depending on density.

160
00:10:06,939 --> 00:10:11,331
For comparison, window glass has an
extinction coefficient below zero point

161
00:10:11,331 --> 00:10:12,736
one inverse centimeters.

162
00:10:12,736 --> 00:10:18,064
The scattering is not a defect — it is a
direct consequence of the porous

163
00:10:18,064 --> 00:10:22,783
Some applications exploit it: aerogel
panels in skylights diffuse sunlight

164
00:10:22,783 --> 00:10:25,269
without the heat gain of clear glazing.

165
00:10:25,269 --> 00:10:27,626
Sound propagates slowly through aerogel.

166
00:10:27,626 --> 00:10:32,161
The speed of sound in low-density silica
aerogel can be as low as one hundred

167
00:10:32,161 --> 00:10:36,697
meters per second, compared to three
hundred forty-three meters per second in

168
00:10:36,697 --> 00:10:41,115
air and five thousand nine hundred meters
per second in solid silica glass.

169
00:10:41,115 --> 00:10:44,955
The reduction occurs because sound waves
must travel through both the solid

170
00:10:44,955 --> 00:10:48,693
skeleton and the air in the pores, and the
two phases are poorly coupled.

171
00:10:48,693 --> 00:10:53,016
The silica network is sparse and the pores
are not aligned, so the path length for

172
00:10:53,016 --> 00:10:56,389
mechanical waves is much longer than the
straight-line distance.

173
00:10:56,389 --> 00:11:01,432
This tortuous geometry also gives aerogel
a high acoustic impedance mismatch with

174
00:11:01,432 --> 00:11:05,355
air, making it an effective sound barrier
despite its low mass.

175
00:11:05,355 --> 00:11:10,915
Measurements by NIST show that aerogel
panels can achieve sound transmission loss

176
00:11:10,915 --> 00:11:13,936
values comparable to much heavier
materials.

177
00:11:13,936 --> 00:11:19,412
The acoustic behavior is another emergent
property of the nanoporous architecture —

178
00:11:19,412 --> 00:11:23,040
the same structure that blocks heat also
impedes sound.

179
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:26,448
The pore size distribution in aerogel is
not fixed.

180
00:11:26,448 --> 00:11:29,723
It can be tuned by changing synthesis
conditions.

181
00:11:29,723 --> 00:11:34,859
A higher precursor concentration produces
smaller pores and a denser network.

182
00:11:34,859 --> 00:11:40,061
The choice of catalyst also matters: acid
catalysis tends to yield finer, more

183
00:11:40,061 --> 00:11:45,264
uniform structures, while base catalysis
produces coarser networks with larger

184
00:11:45,264 --> 00:11:45,664
pores.

185
00:11:45,664 --> 00:11:50,112
Gelation time and temperature affect how
much the silica particles aggregate before

186
00:11:50,112 --> 00:11:51,344
the network solidifies.

187
00:11:51,344 --> 00:11:56,884
Longer gelation times allow more particle
growth and coarsening, resulting in larger

188
00:11:56,884 --> 00:11:57,280
pores.

189
00:11:57,280 --> 00:12:02,200
The range already stated — twenty to one
hundred nanometers — reflects typical

190
00:12:02,200 --> 00:12:04,912
outcomes across these parameter
variations.

191
00:12:04,912 --> 00:12:09,863
Small-angle X-ray scattering and nitrogen
adsorption isotherms are the standard

192
00:12:09,863 --> 00:12:12,181
methods for characterizing pore size.

193
00:12:12,181 --> 00:12:18,813
ASTM International provides test standards
for measuring surface area and pore volume

194
00:12:18,813 --> 00:12:19,749
in aerogels.

195
00:12:19,749 --> 00:12:24,503
The ability to control pore size is
important for applications: smaller pores

196
00:12:24,503 --> 00:12:29,257
improve thermal insulation by further
restricting gas-phase conduction, while

197
00:12:29,257 --> 00:12:33,269
larger pores reduce optical scattering and
increase transparency.

198
00:12:33,269 --> 00:12:38,242
The synthesis is not a single recipe — it
is a parameter space where structure

199
00:12:38,242 --> 00:12:40,283
follows from processing choices.

200
00:12:40,283 --> 00:12:45,063
Aerogel is made of silicon dioxide
arranged as a skeletal network, with air

201
00:12:45,063 --> 00:12:46,912
occupying most of the volume.

202
00:12:46,912 --> 00:12:51,853
The material exists because supercritical
drying removes liquid without surface

203
00:12:51,853 --> 00:12:52,354
tension.

204
00:12:52,354 --> 00:12:57,545
The result is a solid with properties that
follow from its structure — low density,

205
00:12:57,545 --> 00:13:01,111
low thermal conductivity, high porosity,
and brittleness.

206
00:13:01,111 --> 00:13:04,051
The chemistry is ordinary; the geometry is
not.
